  "verified_against": {
    "source": "release-manifest.json",
    "agrees": true,
    "note": "Hashes were downloaded from the published v5.26.0 release assets and hashed independently. v5.26.0 stops a bare `install` (no --mcp) from silently revoking the recorded MCP selection: on a live installation that had stripped the context7, engram and codebase-memory-mcp permissions from all thirteen agents, deleted three convention files and cleared the journal bindings, while the servers stayed configured so nothing looked wrong. The asymmetry was documented on both sides -- `update` reconstructs the selection from the journal precisely because a bare reinstall retires what is not repeated -- and the defence had been added to one side only. Now install over a recorded selection refuses, writes nothing, and names three remedies; --mcp carries three meanings (absent refuses, an empty selection proceeds, a named selection is that selection), --mcp none spells the empty one as a CLI-only spelling, and --dry-run is exempt because it writes nothing. It also fixes the TUI models screen, which stored a model assignment without saying that the rendered configuration does not carry it until an install or update runs -- so the screen reported a model the running agent was not using. A third fix, pruning the directory a retired file leaves empty, was built, reviewed and deliberately pulled: a symlink in the ancestor chain let it delete directories outside the installation, and its boundary tests were green only because they ran against an in-memory filesystem double that has no symlinks. Only the `pegasus` artifact changed; install.sh and the two builder scripts are byte-identical to v5.25.1, so DARQ's own installer does not change in this re-pin."
  }
}
